1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,676
AI can sound certain and still be wrong.

2
00:00:01,676 --> 00:00:03,353
Finly does not give it the account keys.

3
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:07,857
Finly is a trading bot that uses real market prices and virtual money.

4
00:00:07,857 --> 00:00:10,506
AI studies the market and explains a trade.

5
00:00:10,506 --> 00:00:13,817
Fixed code caps the loss and decides what reaches Alpaca.

6
00:00:14,017 --> 00:00:17,538
When signals agree, Finly builds the paper order. AI explains it;

7
00:00:17,538 --> 00:00:19,138
only rules can approve it.

8
00:00:19,338 --> 00:00:21,154
When signals conflict, Finly does nothing.

9
00:00:21,154 --> 00:00:23,575
It shows why and stops before the account.

10
00:00:23,775 --> 00:00:27,153
Every decision carries a receipt: the proposed position,

11
00:00:27,153 --> 00:00:29,687
dollars at risk, and checks passed.

12
00:00:29,687 --> 00:00:35,176
This checked S&P 500 options example risked at most $366—below Finly's $500 limit.

13
00:00:35,176 --> 00:00:38,976
It was a test case, not a live fill.

14
00:00:39,176 --> 00:00:45,292
Finly's separate rules-based base portfolio then ran during live market hours.

15
00:00:45,292 --> 00:00:47,517
At the first close,

16
00:00:47,517 --> 00:00:55,302
it gained $95.32 while SPY lost $57.99—a $153.31 advantage from the same $100,000 start.

17
00:00:55,502 --> 00:01:02,149
We also replayed that base-portfolio rule from 2013 to 2026.

18
00:01:02,149 --> 00:01:10,126
$10,000 became $106,711 with Finly—$38,629 more than SPY after modeled trading costs.

19
00:01:10,326 --> 00:01:15,023
A simpler version of the portfolio was tested on a separate 80-year market record.

20
00:01:15,023 --> 00:01:21,063
It returned 13.37% a year versus 9.48% for the market and stayed ahead at every tested rebalance date.

21
00:01:21,263 --> 00:01:24,562
Judges can open the decision, rerun tests, inspect the Alpaca order,

22
00:01:24,562 --> 00:01:26,961
and verify every number in the public repository.
